    "setup_id": "05601a00c6bc4954635863826740b7b61d18798ad2f99e32a9fef415a652990f",
    "text": "To find the minimum value of the expression \\(\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz}\\), we start by applying the method of the Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean Inequality (AM-GM Inequality). The AM-GM Inequality states that for any non-negative real numbers \\(a, b, c\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a + b + c}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{abc}\n\\]\n\nwith equality if and only if \\(a = b = c\\).\n\nWe apply the AM-GM Inequality to the terms \\(x^3\\), \\(2y^3\\), and \\(4z^3\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{x^3 \\cdot 2y^3 \\cdot 4z^3} = \\sqrt[3]{8x^3 y^3 z^3} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot (xyz) = 2\\sqrt[3]{2} \\cdot (xyz)\n\\]\n\nThis gives us:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{3} \\geq 2\\sqrt[3]{2} \\cdot (xyz)\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by 3, we get:\n\n\\[\nx^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3 \\geq 6\\sqrt[3]{2} \\cdot (xyz)\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz} \\geq 6\\sqrt[3]{2}\n\\]\n\nTo check if this minimum value can be achieved, we need to see if equality in the AM-GM Inequality holds. Equality in AM-GM holds if and only if \\(x^3 = 2y^3 = 4z^3\\). Let's set \\(x^3 = 2y^3 = 4z^3 = k\\). Then we have \\(x^3 = k\\), \\(2y^3 = k\\), and \\(4z^3 = k\\). Solving these equations, we get \\(x = \\sqrt[3]{k}\\), \\(y = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{k}{2}}\\), and \\(z = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{k}{4}}\\).\n\nSubstituting these into the expression \\(\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz}\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k + k + k}{\\sqrt[3]{k} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{k}{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{k}{4}}} = \\frac{3k}{\\sqrt[3]{k \\cdot \\frac{k}{2} \\cdot \\frac{k}{4}}} = \\frac{3k}{\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{k^3}{8}}} = \\frac{3k}{\\frac{k}{2\\sqrt[3]{2}}} = \\frac{3k \\cdot 2\\sqrt[3]{2}}{k} = 6\\sqrt[3]{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\(\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz}\\) is indeed \\(6\\sqrt[3]{2}\\). Therefore, the minimum value is \\(\\boxed{6\\sqrt[3]{2}}\\).",
